WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.261
A quiet Sunday morning.

2
00:00:04.261 --> 00:00:11.524
Card readers in 2,000 coffee shops stay connected to the payments program all day, ready to send a payment.

3
00:00:11.767 --> 00:00:14.560
At 7 a.m., hardly anyone buys coffee.

4
00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:20.146
The program has almost nothing to do, yet it holds about 200 MB of memory.

5
00:00:20.146 --> 00:00:22.411
Why does waiting cost so much?

6
00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:29.448
The simple way is a restaurant where every table has its own waiter, standing by until the guests decide.

7
00:00:29.733 --> 00:00:34.123
The waiter is a thread, one line of work the operating system runs.

8
00:00:34.123 --> 00:00:35.827
The table is a connection.

9
00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:43.936
Standing there is a blocking read: the kernel, the core of the operating system, puts the thread to sleep until bytes arrive.

10
00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:50.664
Asleep, it uses no CPU, but each thread is a task with memory set aside.

11
00:00:50.664 --> 00:00:55.691
2,000 card readers meant 2,028 threads and about 200 MB.

12
00:00:56.067 --> 00:00:58.354
So try one waiter for every table.

13
00:00:58.354 --> 00:01:06.021
A read can be told never to wait: on an empty connection it fails at once, with an error called EAGAIN, try again.

14
00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:09.935
The waiter runs from table to table: ready yet?

15
00:01:09.935 --> 00:01:10.708
Ready yet?

16
00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:15.347
Here's a test with one connection that answers after one second.

17
00:01:15.347 --> 00:01:17.656
How much CPU does the waiter burn?

18
00:01:17.656 --> 00:01:18.743
Pause and guess.

19
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:23.110
All of it.

20
00:01:23.110 --> 00:01:32.659
A full second, and 1,335,566 failed reads, for 8 bytes the sleeping read got for free.

21
00:01:32.933 --> 00:01:35.347
Ready yet is a fine question once.

22
00:01:35.347 --> 00:01:37.405
Not a million times a second.

23
00:01:37.767 --> 00:01:41.914
We want a call that sleeps until any connection in a set has data.

24
00:01:41.914 --> 00:01:48.639
The first answers, select and poll, take the whole list every time, and the kernel checks every connection.

25
00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:53.711
With 2,000 connections and 3 active, every wake-up checks 2,000.

26
00:01:53.711 --> 00:01:56.949
Still asking every table, just from a chair.

27
00:01:57.333 --> 00:01:59.350
Now put a bell on every table.

28
00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:05.197
The waiter registers each bell once, sits down, and walks only to the tables that rang.

29
00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:07.056
That's epoll, on Linux.

30
00:02:07.056 --> 00:02:12.240
epoll_ctl registers each connection once, in the kernel's interest list.

31
00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:17.081
When bytes arrive, the kernel itself puts that connection on the ready list.

32
00:02:17.081 --> 00:02:21.964
And epoll_wait sleeps until the ready list has something, then returns only that.

33
00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:22.679
Watch.

34
00:02:22.679 --> 00:02:24.517
10 bytes arrive for c3.

35
00:02:24.517 --> 00:02:29.151
The kernel puts c3 on the ready list and wakes the thread.

36
00:02:29.151 --> 00:02:30.909
epoll_wait returns c3.

37
00:02:31.100 --> 00:02:36.835
The thread reads the 10 bytes, reads again, gets EAGAIN, and goes back to sleep.

38
00:02:36.835 --> 00:02:38.985
It never touched c1, c2 or c4.

39
00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:43.537
A thread living in this wait-and-handle cycle is called an event loop.

40
00:02:43.933 --> 00:02:48.127
The bell can ring in two ways, and the difference bites.

41
00:02:48.127 --> 00:02:51.797
10 KB arrive on c4, and the loop reads only 4 KB.

42
00:02:52.067 --> 00:02:58.431
By default, epoll is level-triggered: the bell keeps ringing while the table has anything left to order.

43
00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:03.229
So the next epoll_wait returns c4 again, and the loop reads the rest.

44
00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:07.512
Edge-triggered rings once, each time new data arrives.

45
00:03:07.512 --> 00:03:11.449
Same 10 KB, same 4 KB read, and the loop waits again.

46
00:03:11.733 --> 00:03:14.162
What happens to the other 6 KB?

47
00:03:14.162 --> 00:03:15.416
Pause and guess.

48
00:03:18.633 --> 00:03:19.706
They sit there.

49
00:03:19.706 --> 00:03:22.423
They aren't new, so epoll_wait sleeps.

50
00:03:22.667 --> 00:03:27.682
And the client sent its whole request and waits for an answer, so nothing new will come.

51
00:03:27.682 --> 00:03:30.189
The waiter took half the order and sat down.

52
00:03:30.433 --> 00:03:34.551
Edge-triggered wakes the loop less often, which some servers want.

53
00:03:34.551 --> 00:03:38.169
The rule for that code: on every event, read until EAGAIN.

54
00:03:38.633 --> 00:03:40.711
Here the restaurant picture breaks.

55
00:03:40.711 --> 00:03:46.113
A bell is free, but every connection in the interest list costs the kernel a little memory.

56
00:03:46.367 --> 00:03:50.996
And waiters are expensive, while threads are cheap until there are thousands.

57
00:03:50.996 --> 00:03:53.401
Ten card readers can have a thread each.

58
00:03:53.401 --> 00:03:55.987
Thousands of quiet ones want an event loop.

59
00:03:56.333 --> 00:03:59.104
So why did a quiet Sunday cost 200 MB?

60
00:03:59.104 --> 00:04:02.384
Every connection had its own sleeping waiter.

61
00:04:02.667 --> 00:04:10.033
epoll is one waiter and a bell on every table: sign up once, sleep, and walk only to the tables that rang.

62
00:04:10.333 --> 00:04:11.498
One quick check.

63
00:04:11.498 --> 00:04:14.046
2,000 connections, and 3 have data.

64
00:04:14.046 --> 00:04:18.560
How many does poll check, and how many does epoll_wait return?

65
00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:19.725
Pause and think.

66
00:04:22.967 --> 00:04:24.850
poll checks all 2,000.

67
00:04:24.850 --> 00:04:27.417
epoll_wait returns just the 3.
